+# Flight 93 emergency-call recording: a documented custody lead
+
+Research note · 2026-11-14 UTC · telephone track AUD016-A1. This study examined the sources identified below; it did **not** examine the call audio, carrier records, or the original physical evidence receipt.
+
+## Observations, kept in their source layers
+
+1. A September 17, 2001 FBI memorandum says an original tape recording of Edward Felt's cellular 911 call **had been entered into evidence** and that a transcript was enclosed. This is the memorandum's report of a custody event, not independent verification of the physical item, its present location, or its complete chain of custody. [Released Commission packet, physical PDF page 88](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%207%20Files/Team%207%20Box%2012/T7%20B12%20DOJ%20Doc%20Req%2035-13%20Packet%204%20Fdr-%20Entire%20Contents-%20Response%20Letters-%20Reports%20403.pdf#page=88), extracted lines 27–31. The scan's PDF SHA-256 is `7ed35efb7825f26cb0c712d334a8b286c74bcc25c5b44e01f227059f446c6dba`.
+2. The packet also contains an FBI FD-302 dated September 11, 2001 which calls its attached account a verbatim transcription of a recorded emergency call concerning Flight 93. [Same packet, physical PDF page 90](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%207%20Files/Team%207%20Box%2012/T7%20B12%20DOJ%20Doc%20Req%2035-13%20Packet%204%20Fdr-%20Entire%20Contents-%20Response%20Letters-%20Reports%20403.pdf#page=90), extracted lines 11–15. The label describes the document's claim; this review did not listen to the recording or certify the transcription. The PDF contains other pages beyond the two inspected here.
+3. The National Park Service's [Flight 93 phone-call page](https://www.nps.gov/flni/learn/historyculture/phone-calls-from-flight-93.htm) identifies the Felt call with the Westmoreland County 911 center, labels it **9:58 EDT** and **70 seconds**, and presents a transcript attributed to the FBI. The page says its times are Eastern Daylight Time. The captured page, accessed November 14, 2026 UTC, has SHA-256 `3521bd59a1c42b8f33a449465336597ce493ae2a64693ee289bd802b9d40f076`. These are NPS's reported values; the cited FBI memorandum and FD-302 pages do not themselves establish those precise time and duration values. This study did not compare the complete transcripts or verify an exact source-copy chain.
+
+## Relationships and limits
+
+The NPS attributes its transcript to the FBI; it should not be counted as an independent recording or as independent corroboration of every word. The memo's contemporaneous report that a tape entered evidence is evidence against a broad claim that no such recording was ever reported, but it does not establish that the tape still exists or is complete. A transcription may contain mistakes or omissions; the audio and documented production history are needed to test it. The NPS time and duration need underlying dispatch or carrier records and a clock basis for independent verification.
+
+This Westmoreland account, the separately documented FAA Oak Brook reports, and the [Airfone surveillance-center account of a call to a local 911 operator](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/6d17485a4491c3330aab6e9d55e8be0b39da9a32/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-aosc-local-911-2026-11-14/README.md) are different source observations. No record examined here connects the latter accounts to the Felt call; no common caller, call, route, or causal relationship is assumed. The available observations cannot establish an intentional alteration, concealment, or conspiracy.
+
+## Competing explanations and discriminating records
+
+**Untested possibilities:** the cited tape may survive, may have been disposed of, or its present status may be undocumented; the transcript may accurately reflect the recording or contain errors. None is established by this study. The contemporaneous FBI evidence-entry statement is a concrete starting point for checking each possibility.
+
+**TODOs:** identify and inspect the original tape or authenticated digital preservation copy; evidence receipt and item identifier; transfers, examination records, retention decisions and disposition; the transcription's source/version history; Westmoreland dispatch event and routing records; and call-specific carrier records with clock definitions. Compare the audio and independent records with the transcript, 9:58 time and 70-second duration if acquired. No outreach or new records request was made.
+
+## Provenance, controls and prior records
+
+The [source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json) record acquisition dates, SHA-256 values, and scope. `VERIFY.py` checks the retained source hashes and selected source-language anchors; it is an integrity and transcription-locator check, not audio authentication or a historical truth test. The report is a new additive study; earlier conflicting accounts and reports remain unchanged. Raw scans and extracted contact details are not included in this publication.
